1e:I[470545,["/_next/static/chunks/9f06f549534c8a5e.js","/_next/static/chunks/276fa16bd4cf020e.js","/_next/static/chunks/87128f2527464d5c.js","/_next/static/chunks/2d904702e4d1b18c.js","/_next/static/chunks/b1a4b4a170e41847.js","/_next/static/chunks/f9b9d3a69abee8ee.js","/_next/static/chunks/358e760bd3164198.js","/_next/static/chunks/ea566679d384b192.js","/_next/static/chunks/7fae7bca3e4de959.js","/_next/static/chunks/eac00b17670dc9a8.js","/_next/static/chunks/5b7085386fe8ed14.js","/_next/static/chunks/079f70de56a2a4d9.js","/_next/static/chunks/a0c10228c37fdc14.js","/_next/static/chunks/8480234093eeb56c.js","/_next/static/chunks/c73abd7be8c311cf.js","/_next/static/chunks/adf525ed1f944be0.js","/_next/static/chunks/ed17766d5eadb297.js","/_next/static/chunks/65d84b2239f181ae.js","/_next/static/chunks/af1c46f0af80dd0c.js","/_next/static/chunks/a05dae2f3292eb60.js","/_next/static/chunks/19913707827f8dfb.js"],"default"]
1f:T45d,There are two ways to stop a sequence:

| | Immediate stop | End-of-cycle stop |
|---|---|---|
| Principle | **reinitialise** the GRAFCET: all steps are cleared and the initial step is reactivated | a run condition in the receptivity of the transition that **leaves** the initial step |
| Trigger | a stop button, a fault | a run switch that goes false |
| Effect on the steps | immediate return to the initial step | the current cycle finishes, the sequence stops in the initial step, a rest state with no action |
| Effect on the outputs | continuous outputs drop, stored outputs and counters are not cleared | continuous outputs drop at the end of the cycle, nothing is forced |
| Typical use | safety stop, fault | normal production stop |

Place the run condition on the start of the cycle: the machine finishes its cycle and comes to rest in the initial step, where no action is commanded. Stopping in another step would keep its actions commanded. This does not apply when staying in the last step is intended because it is the safe state of the installation (the traffic light of the next exercise stays red).7:["$","$L17",null,{"maxWidth":"lg","sx":{"my":4},"children":["$L18",["$","$L19",null,{"variant":"h2","component":"h1","color":"primary","gutterBottom":true,"children":"Module A5: From GRAFCET to Ladder (part 2)"}],["$","$L19",null,{"variant":"h5","component":"p","color":"text.secondary","children":"Advanced structures"}],["$","$L1a",null,{"sx":{"my":2}}],["$","$L1b",null,{"children":[["$","$L1c",null,{"label":"Overview and prerequisites","children":["$","$L1d",null,{"sx":[{"& p":{"my":1,"lineHeight":1.7},"& p:first-of-type":{"mt":0},"& ul, & ol":{"pl":3,"my":1},"& li":{"my":0.5},"& code":{"backgroundColor":"rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.06)","borderRadius":"4px","px":"4px","fontSize":"0.9em"}},{"color":"text.secondary","& p":{"textAlign":"justify"}}],"dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"<p>This module completes module A4 with the richer structures of a GRAFCET: OR and AND divergences, stopping a sequence, then the limits of one network per transition and the three-block method. Prerequisite: module A4, including the <code>Automatic_cycle</code> and <code>Outputs</code> programs that all the exercises reuse.</p>\n"}}]}],["$","$L1e",null,{"steps":[{"id":"theory-or-divergence-ladder","kind":"theory","title":"OR divergence and convergence","body":["### OR divergence and convergence\n\nAn OR **divergence** is a crossroads: after a step, the sequence splits into several paths (the **branches**) and only one is taken. Example: at the car park barrier, the entry sequence starts if a car asks to enter OR the exit sequence if a car asks to leave.\n\nIn Ladder, nothing special: **one network per branch** in `Automatic_cycle`, and each transition does its own reset and its own set. An OR **convergence** closes the branches: the downstream step is activated by either transition, each in its own network.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/divergences-en.svg\" width=\"480\" alt=\"OR divergence and convergence: step 1, a single bar, two branches each with their own transition and step, then a bar that joins them; AND: one transition, a double bar, two steps started together, then a double bar and a transition\" /></figure></div>","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/divergences-ladder-or-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"GRAFCET with an OR divergence and convergence whose step 1 is initial, and its Ladder networks: the initial step activation (NC contacts X1 to X4 in series, then set of X1), then one network per transition (X1 -> X2, X1 -> X3, X2 -> X4, X3 -> X4), each with the contacts of the upstream step and of the receptivity, then in parallel the reset of the upstream step and the set of the downstream step\" /></figure></div>","### Three special cases of the OR divergence\n\n- **Step skipping**: a branch avoids some steps (here, branch `c` goes from step 1 straight to step 3).\n- **Sequence loop**: a branch goes back, to a step already visited.\n- **Exclusivity**: the branches must not be able to be crossed together. The standard requires all the crossable transitions to be crossed together: two branches then become active at the same time, which is almost never intended: make sure the conditions cannot be true together (for example `a AND NOT b` on one branch and `b AND NOT a` on the other). If two conditions are true together, this Ladder departs from the standard: only the branch whose network is written first is crossed, its reset turning the upstream step off, which the second network then reads (the three-block method, seen at the end of the module, crosses both). Do not rely on this effect, which depends on the order of writing: make the conditions exclusive.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/or-special-cases-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"Three special cases: step skipping where branch c avoids step 2, a sequence loop where branch c goes back to step 1, and exclusivity with the conditions a AND NOT b and b AND NOT a never true together\" /></figure></div>","<details><summary>Going further</summary>\n\nRule 4 of the standard requires all the crossable transitions to be crossed simultaneously, so two OR branches can be active together: the standard does not require exclusivity, it leaves it to the designer to guarantee it. Studomate only flags the obvious cases (two identical receptivities, or one that is always true).\n\n</details>"]},{"id":"exercise-grafcet-or-divergence","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: OR divergence","body":["Implement in Ladder the OR divergence GRAFCET from module A1: a choice between two sequences. Depending on the button pressed, one output or the other turns on, then the system goes back to the initial state.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/exercise-or-divergence-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"380\" alt=\"GRAFCET to implement: initial step 0, an OR divergence, the branch cycle_start1 to step 1 (output1) then end1 back to step 0, and the branch cycle_start2 to step 2 (output2) then end2 back to step 0\" /></figure></div>","**Provided variables**: inputs `cycle_start1` and `cycle_start2` (push buttons), `end1` and `end2` (limit switches); outputs `output1` and `output2`; memories `X0` to `X2`.\n\n**Work to do**:\n\n1. In `Automatic_cycle`, write one network per transition (`X0 -> X1`, `X0 -> X2`, `X1 -> X0`, `X2 -> X0`), then in `Outputs` one network per output. Both programs are provided, empty and called by the Main.\n2. Simulate `cycle_start1` alone, then `cycle_start2` alone.\n3. Press `cycle_start1` and `cycle_start2` at the same time. What happens?","**How to tell it is right**: `cycle_start1` alone activates `X1` and `output1` until `end1`, then the system goes back to `X0`; `cycle_start2` alone does the same with `X2` and `output2`. The analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary>\n\n1. How many transitions leave `X0`? Each has its own network.\n2. Each network sets `X0` back to 0: one reset per transition is enough.\n3. Count 4 transition networks and 2 output networks.\n\n</details>","<details><summary>Show the answer to the question</summary>\n\nOnly one branch is crossed: the one whose network is written first (`X0 -> X1`). Its reset turns `X0` off, and the next network (`X0 -> X2`) reads `X0` already off. The result therefore depends on the order of writing, which is not intended. The exercise does not ask you to fix this, and the provided solution does not fix it. To make the decision explicit, make the receptivities exclusive (`cycle_start1 AND NOT cycle_start2` and `cycle_start2 AND NOT cycle_start1`): no branch is then crossed while both buttons are pressed.\n\n</details>"],"cta":{"templateId":"or-divergence-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"theory-and-divergence-ladder","kind":"theory","title":"AND divergence and convergence","body":["### AND divergence and convergence\n\nAn **AND** divergence starts several branches in parallel: the sequence splits into two paths followed **at the same time**. In Ladder: **a single network** (`X1 -> X2 + X3`): the contacts `X1` and `a` in series, then the reset of `X1` and **two sets** (`X2` and `X3`) in parallel.\n\nThe AND convergence waits for the end of all the branches: a single network `X2 + X3 -> X4`, with the contacts `X2`, `X3` and `c` in series, then the two resets (`X2` and `X3`) and the set of `X4` in parallel.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/divergences-ladder-and-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"GRAFCET with an AND divergence and convergence whose step 1 is initial, and its Ladder networks: the initial step activation (NC contacts X1 to X4 in series, then set of X1), a single network X1 -> X2 + X3 (contacts X1 and a, reset of X1, set of X2 and X3) and a single network X2 + X3 -> X4 (contacts X2, X3 and c, reset of X2 and X3, set of X4)\" /></figure></div>","<details><summary>Going further</summary>\n\nAn AND divergence starts branches in parallel, but the standard does not set their duration: the AND convergence waits for the slowest. If a branch never finishes (missing sensor, fault), the sequence stays blocked at the convergence: this is the danger specific to AND.\n\n</details>"]},{"id":"exercise-grafcet-and-divergence","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: AND divergence","body":["Implement in Ladder the AND divergence GRAFCET from module A1: a cycle start launches two actions in parallel, then the system waits for both to finish.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/exercise-and-divergence-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"320\" alt=\"GRAFCET to implement: initial step 0, transition cycle_start, a double bar, steps 1 (output1) and 2 (output2) in parallel, a double bar, then the transition sensor1 AND sensor2 back to step 0\" /></figure></div>","**Provided variables**: inputs `cycle_start` (push button), `sensor1` and `sensor2`; outputs `output1` and `output2`; memories `X0` to `X2`. The `Automatic_cycle` and `Outputs` programs are provided, empty and called by the Main.\n\n**Work to do**:\n\n1. In `Automatic_cycle`, write one network per transition: `X0 -> X1 + X2` activates `X1` AND `X2`; `X1 + X2 -> X0` requires `X1` AND `X2` AND both sensors, and deactivates both.\n2. In `Outputs`, write one network per output.\n3. Simulate: `cycle_start`, then `sensor1` alone, then `sensor2`.","**How to tell it is right**: after `cycle_start`, `X1` and `X2` are active together and both outputs are true. With `sensor1` alone, nothing changes; as soon as `sensor1` and `sensor2` are both true, `X1` and `X2` turn off, `X0` becomes true again and the outputs turn off, whatever the order of the sensors. The analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary>\n\n1. A single transition activates both steps: a single `X0 -> X1 + X2` network, with two sets.\n2. The convergence is a single network `X1 + X2 -> X0`, which puts `X1`, `X2` and the two sensors in series, and does two resets.\n3. Test in the other order: `sensor2` first, then `sensor1`.\n\n</details>","### Summary: OR and AND\n\n| | OR divergence | AND divergence |\n|---|---|---|\n| Paths | only one is taken (a choice) | all are started (in parallel) |\n| Networks | one per branch | a single one for the whole divergence |\n| Set | each transition activates its branch | a single transition activates all the branches |\n| Convergence | either transition activates the downstream step | a single transition requires all upstream steps and deactivates them all |\n| Danger | two conditions true together: in Ladder, only the branch written first is crossed (the standard crosses both) | a branch that never finishes blocks what follows |"],"cta":{"templateId":"and-divergence-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"theory-grafcet-reset","kind":"theory","title":"Stopping a sequence","body":["$1f","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/stop-methods-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"Two GRAFCETs side by side on the same sequence: on the left, the button stop goes straight back to step 0 from steps 1 and 2; on the right, the run condition start is added to the first transition (a AND start) and the sequence stays at step 0 while start is false\" /></figure></div>","### Where to place the reset network\n\nThe reset must have **the last word** over the normal evolution: place it in `Automatic_cycle`, **after** the transition networks. The Main then calls `Outputs`, which therefore reads steps that are already reinitialised. It has two networks, both under the stop condition: all the resets of the steps, then the set of the initial step. Since the last network that writes a memory wins, even if a transition has just been crossed, the steps restart from the initial step.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/reset-network-order-en.svg\" width=\"480\" alt=\"Execution order: in Automatic_cycle, one network per transition then the reset on stop, then the Outputs program; the last network that writes a memory wins, so the reset placed after the transitions has the last word\" /></figure></div>","> **Warning**: reinitialising the steps clears neither stored outputs (S coils), nor counters, nor numeric variables. For example, in the car park, `spaces` keeps its value after a stop. What must be cleared is handled explicitly, with an R coil or an assignment.","<details><summary>Going further</summary>\n\nIn the IEC 60848 standard, the reinitialisation is written as a **forcing order** `F/G1:{0}`: the GRAFCET G1 is forced into the situation where only the initial step 0 is active. The run and stop modes of a machine (normal production, end-of-cycle stop, emergency stop, restart...) are organised by the **GEMMA** (Guide d'Étude des Modes de Marches et d'Arrêts, a French method for run and stop modes).\n\n</details>"]},{"id":"exercise-grafcet-reset","kind":"exercise","title":"Exercise: traffic light with stops","body":["Implement in Ladder the traffic light GRAFCET from module A1, then add the two stops from the previous step. **Goal**: implement both types of stop.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/exercise-traffic-light-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"500\" alt=\"GRAFCET to implement: step 0 (green), t_green.Q, step 1 (amber), t_amber.Q, step 2 (red), then t_red.Q AND start back to step 0; in orange, the stop that brings steps 1 and 2 back to step 0\" /></figure></div>","**Provided variables**: outputs `green`, `amber` and `red`; inputs `stop` (push button wired normally closed: true at rest, false when pressed) and `start` (switch); memories `X0` to `X2`. The `Automatic_cycle` and `Outputs` programs are provided, empty and called by the Main.\n\n**Work to do**:\n\n1. **Part 1, base program**: green 5 s, amber 2 s, red 5 s, looping, with three TON timers (for example `t_green`, `t_amber` and `t_red`).\n2. **Part 2, immediate stop**: `stop` reinitialises the GRAFCET (steps cleared, initial step reactivated).\n3. **Part 3, end-of-cycle stop**: the `start` switch is added to the receptivity of the last transition (`t_red.Q AND start`). The light finishes its cycle and stays red while `start` is false; when `start` becomes true again, it restarts on green. This differs from the general rule (condition on the start of the cycle): red is the safe state of a junction, so staying there is intended.","**How to tell it is right**: in simulation,\n\n- with `start` true, the light runs green 5 s, amber 2 s, red 5 s then starts again, one light at a time;\n- a press on `stop` during amber goes straight back to step 0: `amber` turns off and `green` turns on;\n- if `start` goes false during green, the light continues to red then stays red; when `start` becomes true again, it restarts on green;\n- the analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary>\n\n1. Write part 1 first and test it, without the stops. Each TON goes in the network of its transition, right after the step contact.\n2. The reset goes in `Automatic_cycle`, after the transition networks: one network clears all the steps, another reactivates `X0`.\n3. `stop` is wired normally closed: which contact do you read, NO or NC? For the end-of-cycle stop, `start` is added to the contacts of the network `X2 -> X0`.\n\n</details>"],"cta":{"templateId":"traffic-light-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"synthesis-parking","kind":"synthesis","title":"Synthesis: car park","body":["Implement in Ladder the control of a 4-space car park, a simplified version of the car park from module A1 with a single barrier: the GRAFCET below is the one to implement.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/parking-station-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"Top view of the car park: a road with the barrier in the middle, the car_present cell under it, the sensors entry_request on the entry side and exit_request on the exit side, and a panel with the full indicator and the spaces counter from 0 to 4\" /></figure></div>","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/parking-command-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"Control GRAFCET: from step 0, an OR divergence; entry_request AND spaces < 4 leads to step 1 (barrier, spaces := spaces + 1), then car_present to step 2 (barrier), then NOT car_present to step 0; exit_request AND spaces > 0 leads to step 3 (barrier, spaces := spaces - 1), then car_present to step 4 (barrier), then NOT car_present to step 0\" /></figure></div>","**Provided variables**: inputs `entry_request` and `exit_request` (a car waits at the entry or the exit: push buttons), `car_present` (cell under the barrier, true while a car is there) and `stop` (stop button wired normally closed); outputs `barrier` (opening) and `full` (indicator); integer memory `spaces`; memories `X0` to `X4`.\n\n**Already provided**: the signalling GRAFCET, which turns on `full` when `spaces` reaches 4, is already implemented in the `Signalling` subprogram, called by the **Main**. You create the `Automatic_cycle` and `Outputs` programs and call them from the Main, after `Signalling`. The effort is on the control and the reset. There is no 3D scene in this exercise: the buttons `entry_request`, `exit_request` and `car_present` in the watch table play the role of the cars.","**Work to do**:\n\n1. In `Automatic_cycle`, implement the control: one network per transition (`X0 -> X1` to `X4 -> X0`).\n2. In `Outputs`, write the output `barrier`, commanded by steps 1 to 4.\n3. On `entry_request` if `spaces < 4`: open the barrier and increment `spaces` **just once** per vehicle (P contact on the step, in `Outputs`).\n4. On `exit_request` if `spaces > 0`: open the barrier and decrement `spaces` just once.\n5. Add a reset of the control on `stop`, in `Automatic_cycle`.\n6. The barrier must never close while `car_present` is true, even after a press on `stop`.\n7. Questions: what happens to `spaces` after a stop? Should it be cleared, and when? What happens if `entry_request` and `exit_request` are true at the same time? Is it acceptable?","**How to tell it is right**: in simulation,\n\n- after 4 entries in a row, `spaces` is 4, `full` turns on and a 5th car does not open the barrier;\n- after an exit, `spaces` decreases by 1 and `full` turns off; `spaces` never goes below 0;\n- `spaces` changes **just once** per vehicle, even if step 1 stays active for several scans (the car is slow to pass) or if `entry_request` stays true;\n- the barrier stays open while `car_present` is true;\n- a press on `stop` during a passage brings the control back to `X0`, but the barrier stays open until the passage ends;\n- the analysis panel reports no error.","<details><summary>Hints</summary>\n\n1. Two sequences leave `X0`: two transition networks (`X0 -> X1` and `X0 -> X3`), as for the OR divergence. The conditions `spaces < 4` and `spaces > 0` are compare blocks.\n2. `spaces := spaces + 1` is a numeric assignment: a P contact on `X1` followed by a Calc block, in `Outputs` (step \"Actions in Ladder: edge and numeric\").\n3. The reset is like the traffic light's: one network clears all the steps of the control, another reactivates `X0`.\n\n</details>","<details><summary>Show the answer to the question</summary>\n\nAfter a stop, `spaces` keeps its value: reinitialising a GRAFCET only touches its steps. This is intended here, because the cars already parked are still there. You would only clear `spaces` for an explicit reset of the installation, for example an initialisation button with the car park empty.\n\nIf `entry_request` and `exit_request` are true together, only the entry branch (written first) is crossed: the exit request, a push button, is lost. To lose no request, make the conditions exclusive or serve the two requests one after the other.\n\n</details>"],"cta":{"templateId":"parking-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Open the editor","solutionLabel":"Solution"}},{"id":"theory-going-further","kind":"theory","title":"Going further: the limits of one network per transition","body":["A network per transition is enough for most GRAFCETs, including all those in modules A4 and A5. This optional step shows where it reaches its limit, on an example: the gate from module A2, driven this time by a **single-button remote**. The next step presents the method that goes beyond it.","### The limits of a network per transition\n\nThe networks run from top to bottom, and each one reads what the previous ones have just written. When a transition is crossed, the next step is active **right away** for the networks written further down: if its own transition is also true, it is crossed in the same scan, and so on. Two consequences:\n\n- an always-true receptivity chains the transitions without the intermediate step being visible (step `X3` of the linear sequence), and its edge or stored actions are lost;\n- one event read by several consecutive transitions crosses them all: a button press, which lasts only one scan, can move the GRAFCET forward by several steps, even bring it back to its starting point.\n\nThis only matters if the GRAFCET really allows it. The single-button gate is the extreme case: the same press opens, stops and restarts, and these transitions form a loop (opening, stop, closing, stop, opening...). No order of writing the networks fixes every case.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/gate-remote-states-en.svg\" width=\"540\" alt=\"Five positions of the gate on its rail between the limit switches limit_closed and limit_open: closed (limit_closed true), opening (open true), open (limit_open true), closing (close true) and stopped mid-travel (open and close false)\" /></figure></div>","**Vocabulary**: a **limit switch** is a switch the gate actuates when it reaches the end of its travel. The **photocell** (variable `photocell`) sends a light beam that detects an obstacle: it is **cut** (variable true) when someone or something passes through the beam. A press **on an edge** means detecting the instant of the press (P contact): a long press counts only once.\n\n**Project variables**: inputs `remote` (push button, a single button), `stop` (stop button wired normally closed), `limit_open` and `limit_closed` (true when the gate is at the end of its travel) and `photocell`; outputs `open` and `close`.","### The gate GRAFCET\n\n| Situation | Event | Result |\n|---|---|---|\n| Closed | press on `remote` | the gate opens |\n| Open | press | the gate closes |\n| Moving | press | the gate stops |\n| Stopped mid-travel | press | it restarts in the opposite direction of the last movement |\n| Moving | `stop` button | the same stop as a press |\n| Closing | photocell cut | the gate stops and reopens |\n\nThe gate stops by itself at the limit switches. While opening or when stopped, the photocell is ignored.\n\nThis specification gives six steps: `X0` closed (initial), `X1` opening (`open`), `X2` open, `X3` closing (`close`), `X4` stopped while opening and `X5` stopped while closing. A press is a rising edge of `remote` (P contact).","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/gate-grafcet-en.svg\" width=\"560\" alt=\"GRAFCET of the gate: initial step 0 (closed) leads to step 1 (opening, action open) on a press. From step 1, it goes to step 2 (open) on limit_open, or to step 4 (stopped while opening) on (press OR stop actuated) AND NOT limit_open; steps 2 and 4 lead to step 3 (closing, action close) on a press. From step 3, it goes back to step 0 on limit_closed, to step 1 on photocell AND NOT limit_closed, or to step 5 (stopped while closing) on (press OR stop actuated) AND NOT photocell AND NOT limit_closed; step 5 leads to step 1 on a press\" /></figure></div>","### A network per transition\n\nThe project below implements this GRAFCET with one network per transition, in the order of the upstream steps (`X0`, then `X1`, and so on). Closed, a press should open the gate. Nothing useful happens: in the first scan, the same press crosses `X0 -> X1`, then `X1 -> X4` (the gate is \"opening\" and the press is still true), then `X4 -> X3`. The `X3 -> X0` network is written higher in the program: it has already been read and does not see `X3` yet. The scan therefore ends on `X3`, and `close` is commanded for one scan although the gate is closed. In the next scan, `X3 -> X0` is crossed (`limit_closed` is true) and `X0` is active again.","### To observe\n\nThe button below opens the gate programmed with one network per transition (`Automatic_cycle` program). There is no physical model: in the watch table, you play the limit switches (`limit_closed` true at the start, `limit_open` true when the gate has arrived) and the photocell.\n\n1. Closed, press `remote` once and follow steps `X0` to `X5`.\n2. Why does `X0` become active again?"],"embeddedTemplateId":"gate-per-transition-ladder","embeddedWatchOpen":true,"embeddedInitialView":{"type":"ladder"},"cta":{"templateId":"gate-per-transition-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Observe: one network per transition","primaryMode":"solution","autostartSimulation":true}},{"id":"theory-three-blocks","kind":"theory","title":"Going further: the three-block method","body":["This optional step follows on from the previous one: it presents the method that avoids chaining several transitions in a single scan, on the remote-controlled gate.","### The three-block method\n\nIt removes the problem by separating the calculation from the evolution. You write, in this order:\n\n1. **The crossing conditions**: one network per transition, `Tn = Xn AND Rn` (the upstream step is active AND the receptivity is true). They are all computed before any step changes.\n2. **The evolution of the steps**: first all the resets (R coils) of all the transitions, then all the sets (S coils).\n3. **The outputs**: one network per output.\n\nBlocks 1 and 2 are two groups of networks in `Automatic_cycle`; block 3 is the `Outputs` program.\n\nEach transition thus reads the state of the steps from the start of the scan: a press crosses only one transition per branch, whatever the order of the networks. The price is one `Tn` memory per transition, read in two places (reset and set).","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/three-blocks-method-en.svg\" width=\"300\" alt=\"The three blocks of networks, in order: crossing conditions and step evolution (Automatic_cycle program), then outputs (Outputs program)\" /></figure></div>","**Why all the resets before all the sets?** A step deactivated by one transition and activated by another on the same scan must stay active: the set, written last, wins over the reset (rule 5 of the IEC 60848 standard). If each transition is handled one after another (its resets then its sets), the reset of `X2` by `T2` comes after the set of `X2` by `T1`: `X2` is wrongly turned off.","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/wrong-order-per-transition-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"Two columns of four networks: handling each transition one after another, X2 is set to 1 by T1 then reset to 0 by T2 and wrongly stays off; writing all the resets before all the sets, X2 is reset to 0 then set to 1 and stays active\" /></figure></div>","<div style=\"text-align:center\"><figure><img src=\"/training/ladder/three-blocks-example-en.svg\" width=\"520\" alt=\"The Ladder networks of the linear sequence in three blocks: T0 to T3 (X contacts in series with cycle_start, end1, end2), then the four R coils of steps X0 to X3 followed by the four S coils of X1, X2, X3 and X0, then output1 driven by X1 and output2 by X2\" /></figure></div>","### To observe\n\nThe button below opens the same gate, programmed with the three blocks. As in the previous step, you play the limit switches and the photocell in the watch table.\n\n1. Closed, a press on `remote` opens the gate (`open` true). A second press stops it, a third makes it restart in the opposite direction.\n2. Compare with the project of the previous step: the number of networks and of memories."],"embeddedTemplateId":"gate-grafcet-ladder","embeddedWatchOpen":true,"embeddedInitialView":{"type":"ladder"},"cta":{"templateId":"gate-grafcet-ladder","exerciseLabel":"Observe: three blocks","primaryMode":"solution","autostartSimulation":true}},{"id":"conclusion","kind":"theory","title":"Conclusion","body":["You can now implement a complete GRAFCET in Ladder. Take stock, notion by notion:\n\n- **divergences**: how many networks for an OR choice? And for an AND parallel split?\n- **stops**: what does the reinitialisation do, what does it not do, and where does it go? When to prefer the end-of-cycle stop?\n- **programs**: how does the car park split the signalling, the evolution and the outputs?\n- **limits**: when does a network per transition reach its limit, and what does the three-block method change?","<details><summary>Show the answers</summary>\n\nAn OR choice has one network per branch, in `Automatic_cycle`; an AND parallel split has a single network for the whole divergence, and a single one for the convergence, which requires all upstream steps. The reinitialisation clears the steps and reactivates the initial step, but touches neither stored outputs nor counters; it goes in `Automatic_cycle`, after the transitions, so that `Outputs`, called next, reads steps that are already reinitialised. The end-of-cycle stop lets the current cycle finish. In the car park, the signalling, the evolution and the outputs form three programs called in this order by the Main. A network per transition reaches its limit when the same condition (a button press, for example) is read by transitions that follow one another in one scan: the three-block method first computes all the conditions, then makes the steps evolve.\n\n</details>","To continue, apply what you have learned to your own projects: the user manual (Help menu) details every function of the editor."]}],"prevLabel":"Previous","nextLabel":"Next","moduleId":"a5","initialStepId":"$undefined","nextModule":"$undefined"}]]}],"$L20","$L21"]}]
